{{also|hátán|hat an}}
==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*haitaną||command, name}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*kei-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ki-||put in motion, be moving}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|hēta}}, {{cog|osx|hētan}}, {{cog|goh|heizzan}}, {{cog|non|heita}} ({{cog|sv|heta}}), {{cog|got|𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of {{cog|grc|κίειν||put in motion}}, {{cog|la|ciere||rouse, make active}} and {{cog|sq|cys||to spur, set in motion}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhɑːtɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|head=hātan}}

# to [[order]], [[command]]
# to [[call]] or [[name]]
# {{lb|ang|intransitive}} to be called or named

